      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND METHODS",
          "content": "There is a growing awareness of the possibility of headache resulting from intracranial air secondary to identification of the epidural space by loss of resistance using air (LOR-A). Most reported cases have been attributed to subarachnoid air passage. A case is described of sudden headache following LOR-A."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Various features of the case suggest that the passage of air to the head was subdural rather than subarachnoid."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Recovery was uneventful."
        }
      ]},
